{# @projects blocking_check.severity, blocking_check.virulence, blocking_check.closure_gen #}
{#
  Migration 0100 — THE FIRST BEHAVIOURAL CLAIM IN THE REPOSITORY.

  ARCHITECTURE.md §5.11 item 1 · §2.2 finding S1 · §16 MI25 · spec/binding/authority-source.md §4.

  Everything up to this file is shape: schemas, roles, types, tables, constraints. This is the
  first file that makes the database DO something a writer did not ask for, and what it does is
  the whole company:

      an agent inserts a blocking check claiming severity = 1 and virulence = 'routine'
      on a clause whose blame closure holds max_severity = 5, and the row that lands
      reads (5, 'blood_fatal').

  Finding S1 in one sentence: `disposition.virulence` used to be copied from
  `blocking_check.virulence`, and `blocking_check.virulence` was an INPUT. An LLM-driven role
  that could write a check could therefore choose the virulence class, and the clearance lattice
  — the mechanism that makes `mechanism_absent` on a fatality-written control a 23503 — would be
  enforcing a claim the writer made about itself. The flagship claim was launderable one hop
  upstream. The fix is not a grant and not a review step: `severity`, `virulence` and
  `closure_gen` become PROJECTIONS, overwritten from the closure REGARDLESS of what the inserter
  supplied, and a missing closure row REFUSES.

  THREE PROPERTIES OF THIS FILE ARE CONTRACTUAL (spec/binding/authority-source.md §4):

    1. The assignment is UNCONDITIONAL. There is no `IF NEW.severity IS NULL` guard, so a
       correct guess confers no privilege and a wrong one is silently corrected rather than
       argued with. A guard would make the column an input again for every writer who bothers
       to leave it null.
    2. The `IF … IS NULL THEN RAISE` block is NOT OPTIONAL. It is emitted from `on_missing`, and
       `on_missing` has exactly one legal value, so the refusal cannot be configured away.
    3. The three `-- @projects` / `-- @authority` / `-- @on_missing` header lines are
       machine-readable, so `trappoint render --check` and `trappoint migrate lint` can verify
       that the committed SQL still corresponds to the declaration that produced it.

  WHY THE RELATION AND THE KEY COME FROM THE BINDING AND THE COLUMN NAMES DO NOT.
  `severity`, `virulence` and `closure_gen` are SUBSTRATE column names — they are named in
  spec/TRAPPOINT-SPEC.md and a vertical may not rename them, exactly as a vertical may not
  rename `person`. The authority RELATION is the vertical's: MAINLINE derives from
  `mainline.clause_blame_current`, the reference vertical from
  `trappoint_ref.clause_blame_current`, and the two sides of the lookup are NAMED DIFFERENTLY
  (`commit_id` on the projected row against `as_of_commit` on the closure), which is why
  `key_columns` exists and why this template reads both.

  THE NULL PROBE IS ON THE FIRST PROJECTED COLUMN, and that is a constraint on the binding, not
  an accident: the first entry of `columns` must be NOT NULL in the authority relation, or a
  present-but-null authority row would read as an absent one. In both shipped bindings that
  column is `max_severity`, declared `INT2 NOT NULL`.

  PLATFORM (measured on CockroachDB CCL v26.2.5, local single node, 2026-08-09):
  a column of `NEW`/`OLD` must be read as `(NEW).col` — the unparenthesised PostgreSQL spelling
  used throughout ARCHITECTURE.md §5.11 CREATES SUCCESSFULLY and then fails at EXECUTION with
  `42P01: no data source matches prefix: new in this context`. A SQLSTATE outside the refusal
  taxonomy, raised the first time the gate is actually exercised, is the worst possible failure
  mode for this file, so every read here is parenthesised. Assignment targets are NOT
  parenthesised (`NEW.severity := …`), which is also measured.
#}
{% set coltype = {
     'severity':    'INT2',
     'virulence':   binding.schema ~ '.virulence_class',
     'closure_gen': 'INT8',
   } %}
{% set ns = namespace(entry=none) %}
{% for a in authority_sources %}
{%   if 'blocking_check.severity' in a.projects %}{% set ns.entry = a %}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% if ns.entry is none %}{{ binding_declares_no_authority_source_for_blocking_check_severity }}{% endif %}
{% set e = ns.entry %}
{% set names = [] %}{% for p in e.projects %}{% set _ = names.append(p.split('.')[1]) %}{% endfor %}
{% set reads = [] %}{% for c in e.columns %}{% set _ = reads.append('c.' ~ c) %}{% endfor %}
{% set intos = [] %}{% for n in names %}{% set _ = intos.append('v_' ~ n) %}{% endfor %}
{% set preds = [] %}{% for kc in e.key_columns %}{% set _ = preds.append('c.' ~ kc ~ ' = (NEW).' ~ e.key[loop.index0]) %}{% endfor %}
-- @file 0100_fn_check_project.sql
{{ header(file='0100_fn_check_project.sql',
          title='CREATE FUNCTION ' ~ binding.schema ~ '.fn_check_project — severity and virulence are projections, never inputs',
          rationale='Finding S1: the flagship claim was launderable one hop upstream, because an LLM-driven role that could write a blocking check could choose its virulence class and the clearance lattice would then enforce a claim the writer made about itself. This function overwrites severity, virulence and closure_gen from the blame closure regardless of what the inserter supplied, and refuses with P0001 when no closure row exists. Absence of evidence refuses; it never defaults.',
          mi=['MI25', 'MI01'], i=['I02']) }}
--
-- migration:  0100_fn_check_project
-- band:       0100-0109z · kernel/projection-triggers · RENDERED
--             Allocated by verticals/mainline/db/migrations.allocation.toml (MR-6 lock 1).
-- statements: 1
-- invariants: MI25 — blocking_check.severity and .virulence are projections of the blame
--             closure, never inputs; raising on a missing closure is the whole of it.
-- source:     ARCHITECTURE.md §5.11 item 1 · §2.2 S1 · §16 MI25 ·
--             spec/binding/authority-source.md §4 (this emission is normative there)
-- requires:   {{ e.relation }} (the binding's declared authority relation) ·
--             {{ binding.schema }}.blocking_check (migration 0058, RENDERED, kernel
--             `obligation-and-clearance`) · {{ binding.schema }}.virulence_class (0013)
-- provides:   {{ binding.schema }}.fn_check_project() — welded to blocking_check by 0120
-- sqlstate:   P0001 when the closure lookup misses. NEVER a synthetic 23514/23503/23505:
--             spec/errors.md §3.3 bans a synthetic constraint-backed code, because it produces
--             an exhibit nobody can name.
-- forward-only; no .down.sql exists at or below the protected floor, and under MR-5 no
--             .up.sql either.
--
-- THE CONTRACT LINES BELOW ARE MACHINE-READABLE. `trappoint render --check` and the migration
-- linter read them to confirm the committed SQL still corresponds to the declaration that
-- produced it, so they are not decoration and they are not free-text.
--
-- @projects {{ e.projects | join(', ') }}
-- @authority {{ e.relation }} ({{ e.key_columns | join(', ') }}) <= NEW ({{ e.key | join(', ') }})
-- @on_missing raise
--
-- WHAT A REVIEWER SHOULD CHECK, IN ORDER:
--   (a) there is no `IF … IS NULL` guard around the assignments — the overwrite is total;
--   (b) the RAISE is P0001 and its message begins '{{ binding.name }}: ';
--   (c) nothing in the body reads a column of the row being inserted except the lookup key.
--       The projected columns are WRITE-ONLY here. If a future edit ever reads
--       `(NEW).severity`, P-2 is broken and the render contract cannot see it.
--
-- ACYCLICITY. BEFORE INSERT on blocking_check, writing only to NEW. It reads one relation it
-- can never write. Trigger depth contributed: 0.

CREATE FUNCTION {{ binding.schema }}.fn_check_project() RETURNS TRIGGER LANGUAGE PLpgSQL AS $$
DECLARE
{% for n in names %}
  v_{{ n }} {{ coltype[n] }};
{% endfor %}
BEGIN
  -- The authority read. One row, addressed by the declared key, from a relation the role that
  -- writes blocking_check may not write (rule A-6, asserted structurally at render time and by
  -- privilege probe at migration time).
  SELECT {{ reads | join(', ') }}
    INTO {{ intos | join(', ') }}
    FROM {{ e.relation }} c
   WHERE {{ preds | join(' AND ') }};

  IF v_{{ names[0] }} IS NULL THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION USING ERRCODE='P0001',
      MESSAGE='{{ binding.name }}: no blame closure for this clause version — cannot arm a check';
  END IF;

  -- UNCONDITIONAL. A supplied value is overwritten whether or not it agrees.
{% for n in names %}
  NEW.{{ n }} := v_{{ n }};
{% endfor %}
  RETURN NEW;
END $$;
